                <description><![CDATA[<article>
				<blockquote class="messageText SelectQuoteContainer ugc baseHtml">
					
					<div style="text-align: center"><br>
<span style="font-size: 9px"><span style="color: #ebcc95"><b>N O T E</b></span></span><br>



<div class="ToggleTriggerAnchor bbCodeSpoilerContainer">
	<button type="button" class="button bbCodeSpoilerButton ToggleTrigger Tooltip JsOnly" data-target="&gt; .SpoilerTarget"><span>Spoiler</span></button>
	<div class="SpoilerTarget bbCodeSpoilerText"><span style="font-size: 12px">With Apple Music exclusives becoming more and more popular and <b>24bit</b> becoming the new norm, it is unsure if labels are sending out true 24bit files for older albums or if they're upscaling them since 44.1khz means <a href="https://www.spek.cc/" target="_blank" class="externalLink" rel="nofollow">Spek</a> and other programs can read 44.1khz that'll look like a lossless 16-bit and it has no way to show if it's more than that without changing the sample rate (which then leaves blank space). As this was made in a time of the world where vinyls were not as popular... more than likely, it's possible this is just another <i>"24bit upscale"</i> from the label. With that being said, I am posting since this in case it's real - these were ripped by me. 24bit is not even recognizable to the human ear, but if you'd like to have this for your downloads, this is for you.</span><br>
<b><span style="font-size: 22px"><span style="text-decoration: underline">I am not responsible if this is a 24-bit upscale provided by the distributors of the files (whoever sends them out via the label), but regardless, I still hope you enjoy.</span></span></b></div>
</div><br>
<img src="https://i.ibb.co/1YsmHmqJ/2000x2000-1.jpg" class="bbCodeImage LbImage" alt="[​IMG]" data-url="https://i.ibb.co/1YsmHmqJ/2000x2000-1.jpg" style=""><br>
